  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the dough, which is $1,400\\,\\text{g}.$ The percentage is $24.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the wheat flour in the dough.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n24.5%=24.5÷100=0.245\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =24.5%\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =0.245\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24.5%$ of $1,400$ is $343.$ Thus, there are $343\\,\\text{g}$ of wheat flour in the dough.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n24.5%=\\frac{24.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =24.5%\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =\\frac{24.5}{100}\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =24.5\\times \\frac{1,400}{100} \\\\  & =24.5\\times 14 \\\\  & =343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24.5%$ of $1,400$ is $343.$ Thus, there are $343\\,\\text{g}$ of wheat flour in the dough.",
